From: Macron <macron@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] winecoreaudio: Put an engine between a shared render stream
 and CoreAudio.

A shared render stream's event is set by a timer of the driver's own, once
a period, while CoreAudio takes the sound straight out of the client's
buffer whenever the device wants some. The two run on different clocks,
and at different intervals when the stream's sample rate is not the
device's: the driver sets the device's buffer to the stream's period in
frames, so a 44100 Hz stream with a 470-frame period on a 48000 Hz device
is pulled every 9.8 ms and asked for more every 10.7 ms.

A client that keeps a period or two queued, as SDL's WASAPI code does
(one chunk written at a wake-up, and only when at most one is queued),
then runs dry by itself. In a model of that client with wake-ups 2 ms late
at most nine times in ten and up to 12 ms otherwise, a 44100 Hz stream on
a 48000 Hz device is short about 450 times a minute, and streams at the
device's rate 2 to 28 times a minute.

Now the event is set when CoreAudio has taken a period's worth of frames,
as Windows sets it when its engine has, and again after a write that
leaves less than a period beyond the reserve. And the stream keeps a
reserve the client is not told about: GetCurrentPadding counts only what
is queued beyond it. The reserve starts at one period, grows by what was
missing whenever CoreAudio found the buffer short while the client was
feeding it (up to 100 ms), and gives back half of what five minutes
without a gap never used. GetStreamLatency includes it. After Stop the
reserve still plays.

Every render stream that played prints one line a minute on stderr with
the frames CoreAudio asked for and did not get, in either mode.

MACRON_AUDIO=upstream leaves streams as they were.
MACRON_AUDIO_RESERVE=<min ms>[,<max ms>] bounds the reserve.
MACRON_AUDIO_REPORT=<seconds> changes how often a stream reports (0: never).
---
